{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## RCA — my framing of this ticket was WRONG. Problem 2 is intended behaviour; Problem 1 is real but did not cause it.\n\n### Problem 2 — \"the gate fails OPEN on a provider error\" — REFUTED\n\nThe completion verifier **ran and PASSED**. The durable sidecar the close emitted proves it:\n\n    .tickets-tracker/e369-a449-4773-48fb/1785827264280828001-...-COMPLETION_VERDICT.json\n      verdict: PASS\n      model:   bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6\n      criteria: 13 evaluated       findings: 0\n      verified_at_sha: 996a3b107f8b78de78a497e6797741d5da42fac2\n\nSo verification was not skipped, not swallowed, and not degraded. The Bedrock haiku 400 came from a\nDIFFERENT caller and was incidental to the close.\n\n**Why no attestation, then.** `gate_ops.py:129` — `certifiable = not uncertified` — with the intent\nstated verbatim above it:\n\n> \"Certification is WITHHELD iff a direct child is closed-but-UNCERTIFIED (force-closed): the parent\n> MAY close (subject to its own criteria) but cannot be certified — certification propagates, so an\n> unattested descendant withholds the parent's signature. **This is a close-vs-certify distinction,\n> NOT a block.**\"\n\n`transition_close.py:409` then returns before signing. Close proceeds, exit 0, no attestation — by\ndesign, and documented at the line.\n\n**The two uncertified descendants, enumerated:**\n\n    0059  bug  DC inbound filing is a no-op in live CI: reconcile r…\n    2bc7  bug  Test helpers return an unregistered copy of mutation…\n\nBoth are `closed` with no `completion-verifier` attestation. All 39 other descendants carry one.\n\n**Ruled out along the way, each with evidence:**\n * gate disabled → `gates.gate_enabled(...) = True` for the epic, identical to a bug that DID certify.\n * duplicate/not-a-bug skip → `_has_live_replacement_link(...) = False`.\n * type-based skip → the skip list is `(\"session_log\",\"code_review\",\"identity\")`; `epic` is absent.\n * verify-vs-sign drift → `_material_drifted` needs the two SHAs to differ; `verified_at_sha` equals\n   the close worktree's HEAD exactly, and the reflog shows HEAD last moved 06:51 UTC, before the\n   close started at ~06:52 (error at 07:05).\n * recovery swallowing the error → `completion_recovery.py:553` re-raises `CompletionRecoveryError`.\n\n**Classification: `intended-behavior`.** Authority: the cited design comment at `gate_ops.py:126-129`\nplus the matching contract in `transition_close.py:405-410`. There is no defect to repair here, and\n\"fixing\" it would break certification propagation — an epic could then be certified while part of\nits subtree is not.\n\n### Problem 1 — the Bedrock 400 — CONFIRMED as a real, separate defect\n\n`review_kernel/verify.py:301 model_max_output_tokens` maps by substring (haiku 64K, sonnet/opus\n128K) and `max_output_cfg` **only ever RAISES**:\n\n    return cfg if cap <= cfg.max_tokens else replace(cfg, max_tokens=cap)\n\nSo a cap raised for a 128K model is carried into a later call that resolves to the 64K haiku and is\nnever re-clamped. Bedrock validates strictly and rejects. Demonstrated as a pure function:\n\n    max_output_cfg(Cfg(sonnet, 16000))            -> max_tokens = 128000\n    replace(that, model=haiku) -> max_output_cfg  -> max_tokens = 128000   (haiku ceiling 64000)\n    PREDICTION IF TRUE 128000 / IF FALSE 64000    -> CONFIRMED\n\nThis violates the function's OWN stated contract — \"Unknown/absent models fall back CONSERVATIVELY\n… so an unmapped model never requests an output larger than its provider accepts\" — which the\nshrinking-model case defeats. Classification: `defect`. Direct Anthropic tolerated it; Bedrock does\nnot, which is why `d2ce` (default local dev to Bedrock) surfaced it.\n\n### What this means for closing e369\n\nThe epic does not need a gate change. It needs its two uncertified descendants certified — reopen\n0059 and 2bc7, move each back to `in_progress`, re-close through the gate — after which a re-close\nof the epic will sign. That is the documented remedy, and it satisfies the operator's rule that a\nclose without completion verification is prohibited."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785856471061746001,"uuid":"c965bc63-44fa-4202-8071-a08ec4180050"}